## 13.4. C. glauca  
Since the late 19th century, C. glauca has been used as a rootstock material for grafting other citrus species. It was introduced into the USA in 1911 and was subsequently distributed to various states in the USA for field trials. While there was no significant uptake among international growers, it continues to be important in citrus breeding programs and is sometimes used as a rootstock.  
Around the turn of the millennium, C. glauca was earmarked as a key emerging bush-food species, due to its flavour, processing properties, and plantation potential. Richmond et al. estimated the annual production at 13 tonnes in 2019, while Tworney et al. reported a retail price of AUD 25–28/kg in 2009. However, production appears to have slowed in recent years. The main commercial cultivars are the Australian Outback and CR101-13.  
The benefits of growing C. glauca, summarised from Douglas and other sources, are provided below:  
(1) Ease of growing;  
(2) Tolerant of frost and heat, so can be grown over a wide climatic area. Cultivated trees have reportedly been grown in all Australian states, including Tasmania;  
(3) Appealing flavour;  
(4) Retains its structure and flavour when frozen;  
(5) The fruit is ‘nonbrowning’, unlike most commercial citrus;

